  "read": {
    "headline": "Brazil rose 8%. Tech hit a record. The ten-year hit 5.35%.",
    "standfirst": "Monday's tape had three stories at once. Brazil's Ibovespa jumped 7.7% to a record 206,921, its biggest one-day gain since March 2020, after right-wing Senator Flavio Bolsonaro finished first in Sunday's presidential vote, 47.0% to President Lula's 45.2%, against polls that had Lula ahead; the real strengthened about 4% through R$5 and the Brazil ETF rose 12.5%. On Wall Street the Nasdaq Composite closed at a record 27,477.31, up 1.05%, led by Nvidia's third straight record, with the S&P 500 up 0.66% to 7,773.95, within 0.3% of its summer high, and the Dow up 0.18%. And bonds sold off again: the ISM services prices-paid index rose to 74.0, the highest since July 2022, and the ten-year touched 5.349%, the highest since 2002, before closing near 5.31%. Oil fell about 2% to $100.3 on Brent on a G7 stock release and Gulf exports at pre-war levels, though tanker attacks continued and Iran called talks meaningless. The euro hit a 17-month low near $1.116 on French and Spanish politics. Asia is higher, the Nikkei near 70,700. Today: the three-year auction, Williams, Musalem, Bowman and Schmid, and the BoJ's Ueda.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "Brazil's ETF rose 12.5% in a day. It was the biggest move on the board by a factor of two.",
        "text": "The Brazil country fund closed at 42.98, up 12.54% on Monday, 15.72% on the week and 13.52% over a month; the Ibovespa rose 7.71% to a record close, the dollar fell about 4.1% to R$5.00, and the one-day move in the Brazil ETF is more than twice the next-largest in the table. The trigger was Sunday's first round: Flavio Bolsonaro took 47.03% of valid votes against Lula's 45.16% where polls had Lula about three points ahead, and his allies gained seats in Congress, which analysts say would make it easier to pass tighter spending, privatisations and tax cuts; the runoff is on 25 October. Itau gained about 15% in New York, Bradesco 19%, Nu Holdings 14%, Petrobras 12% and the exchange operator B3 more than 20%, while the pulp exporter Suzano fell as much as 8.5% on the stronger real. The move spilled into the neighbours: Argentina rose 5.93%, Chile 2.43% and Mexico 1.10%. Brazil's deficit was close to 10% of GDP in June, according to CNBC, the backdrop for investors' interest in a platform of fiscal discipline; the runoff is under three weeks away."
      },
      {
        "title": "A record Nasdaq on a 5.31% ten-year. The two numbers do not usually appear on the same day.",
        "text": "The Nasdaq Composite rose 1.05% and the Nasdaq 100 0.87% to records, with Nvidia up 2.12% to a record $238.90, its third in a row, Microsoft 1.5%, Meta 1.9% and Tesla 2.2%; the equal-weight S&P 500 fund is down 3.6% over a month against 0.7% for the cap-weighted index, which is the concentration in one number. Communication services rose 1.17%, materials 1.31%, energy 1.00%, financials 0.73% and health care 0.72%, with only real estate lower, down 0.34%. Over a month technology is +7.29%, the only sector in the green, against financials −7.26%, utilities −7.22%, real estate −7.42% and materials −5.61%. Among the factors growth beat the index by 0.33%, recent IPOs by 1.40%, momentum lagged by 0.59% and private equity by 1.26%. Micron fell 1.3%. The VIX closed at 15.52. Capital.com's Daniela Hathorn calls it a divergence: growth stocks rally because Fed expectations softened while the risk-free rate against which they are valued stays exceptionally high."
      },
      {
        "title": "Services growth slowed. Services prices did not. The ten-year took the prices.",
        "text": "The ISM services index fell to 54.9 from 55.4 with business activity down to 56.5 from 61.7 and export orders in contraction, but prices paid rose to 74.0, the highest since July 2022, and employment returned above 50. The ten-year touched 5.349% and closed near 5.31%, up about 3bp; the thirty-year touched 5.703% and closed near 5.66%; the two-year held near 4.84%. In the funds the 20-year-plus Treasury fell 0.48% and is down 6.20% over a month and 8.80% over three, the 10–20 year fund 0.42%, the 7–10 year fund 0.15%, mortgage-backed 0.15%; the short end rose 0.04%, municipals 0.16% and emerging-market bonds 0.22%. October's hike is about 20–22% priced, a hold about 76–80%. The tanker rates in the ISM comments are worth noting: a very large crude carrier now costs about $1.6 million a day to charter, 24 times the 2025 rate according to Clarksons, a shipping cost of the kind the survey comments cite alongside fuel and interest expense."
      },
      {
        "title": "Oil fell 2% on Monday. Brent's five-day column still counts the November contract.",
        "text": "December Brent settled near $100.3, down about 1.9%, and WTI near $89.4, down 1.8%; this morning Brent is $100.67 and WTI $89.64, both up about 0.3%. The supports are the G7's 100 million barrel emergency release and its pledge to avoid export bans, the top OPEC+ producers leaving November quotas unchanged, and JPMorgan's estimate that Middle East crude exports are back to 17.5 million barrels a day, 98% of pre-war, with products at 58%. Against that: at least 11 tankers have been attacked in or near Hormuz since last Monday, five incidents were reported on Monday alone, the US blockade of Iranian ports has redirected 130 vessels, Iran's president called talks with Washington meaningless and the Houthis claimed three operations against Saudi airports, an oil facility and military sites. The Brent row's five-day −1.60% still starts on a day when the November contract was the front month, so it understates the December contract's move since Wednesday; heating oil is $4.53, +112.27% this year, and gasoline $3.24 is +88.62%. The API inventory report is due this evening."
      },
      {
        "title": "The euro hit $1.116 and France's spread 141bp. Spain called an election on top.",
        "text": "EUR/USD is 1.12, down 0.08% on the day, 3.40% over a month and 4.60% this year, after touching about $1.116, its lowest since May 2025, on Monday. The French ten-year yield traded near 4.92–5.0%, the premium over Germany reached about 141bp, the widest since 2012, before narrowing to about 137bp, the government conceded a 5.4% deficit this year against a 5% target, and Prime Minister Sanchez called a snap election in Spain; France's CAC fell 0.8% and the euro-area composite PMI rose to 53.1, the fastest in about three and a half years. ING says about 30bp has come out of expected ECB tightening since late September against 13bp for the Fed, and UOB expects a pause in October and a final hike in December. The dollar index is about 102.1; USD/JPY is 158.12, up 0.15%, with EUR/JPY near 177 after seven straight declines; GBP/USD 1.32, the Australian dollar 0.70 and the kiwi near a one-year low. Bitcoin is $85,584, +6.53% over a month and 35.15% over three; Cardano gained 9.59% on the week."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold slipped to $4,150. Cocoa and cotton rose 3.5%. Sugar is up 14.7% in a month.",
        "text": "Gold is $4,150.10, down 0.16%, 1.24% on the week and 7.29% over a month; silver $60.84, platinum $1,715.50 and palladium all lower on the morning, while LME copper rose 1% on Monday to $14,401 a tonne and the US copper contract is $6.65, +1.64% on the week. The agricultural board moved at the margins: cocoa +3.47% to 5,867 and +9.03% on the week, cotton +3.54% to 80.51, lean hogs +1.18%, sugar flat at 20.73 but +9.45% on the week, +14.72% over a month and +36.92% over three. Wheat is +44.10% this year and 49.70% over twelve months; corn is 7.36% lower over a month. Natural gas is $3.07, +3.61% on the week. Over twelve months, the one-year column is back this morning: crude is +45.31%, Brent +53.77%, heating oil +101.80%, and gold +4.37%."
      }
    ]
